<!-- Adaptive-icon foreground: the same shaded 3D crescent the unlock screen shows, not a flat
     silhouette of it.

     Where @drawable/ic_moon_3d comes from (a PNG cannot carry a comment, so it is recorded here
     and referenced from splash_moon.xml, the only other thing that uses it): it is
     feature-auth/res/drawable-nodpi/ic_crescent_moon_3d.png — the 256px Microsoft Fluent Emoji 3D
     1F319, MIT-licensed, the exact file AuthScreen draws — cropped to (15,15)-(241,241) and
     resampled to 384x384 with Lanczos. That crop is the square that bounds the artwork's minimal
     enclosing circle (centre 127.71,127.66, radius 112.09), so in ic_moon_3d the crescent's
     bounding circle IS the square canvas. Resampling was done on premultiplied pixels, because
     the source stores RGB (0,0,0) under its transparent pixels and an unpremultiplied resize
     would have bled black into the anti-aliased rim.

     Because the bounding circle is the canvas, the inset below states the moon's diameter
     directly: 25% off every side leaves 50% of the layer box, i.e. 54dp of the adaptive icon's
     108dp canvas. That is inside the 66dp safe zone every launcher mask is guaranteed to keep, and
     inside the 72dp circle the standard circular mask actually shows, with 25% clearance. 50% is
     also exactly the fraction splash_moon rests at, so the moon is the same relative size on the
     launcher and on the splash — the property the vector this replaced was hand-tuned to get.
     Measured in the emulator's app drawer: a 157px mask disc with the moon reaching 58.2px from
     its centre, i.e. 49.4% of the 108dp canvas and 25.9% clear of the mask edge.

     The inset is a fraction rather than a dp value so it tracks whatever pixel size the launcher
     rasterises the icon at. The bitmap inside an InsetDrawable is drawn with the BitmapDrawable
     default gravity (fill), which stretches it to the inset bounds — uniform here only because
     the adaptive-icon layer box is square.

     Centring is the artwork's own: that enclosing circle is centred at (127.71, 127.66) of a
     256px canvas, i.e. the crescent was drawn optically centred rather than bounding-box centred,
     so a symmetric inset needs no extra nudge. (The vector this replaced had to hand-nudge itself
     2dp left and 1.6dp up for exactly that reason.) -->
<inset xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:drawable="@drawable/ic_moon_3d"
    android:inset="25%" />
